le><h2 style=3D"color:#000;font:700 24px/31px nyt-cheltenham,georgia,serif;=
margin:0 0 10px"><span>=E2=80=98I am a Chinese worker=E2=80=99</span></h2><=
p style=3D"display:block;color:#333;font:400 18.5px/26px georgia,serif;marg=
in:0 0 10px"><span>By Yan Zhuang</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25p=
x georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>In Seoul, where I live, i=
t=E2=80=99s normal to place an online order on Korea=E2=80=99s equivalent o=
f Amazon a little before midnight and have it arrive at your door before 7 =
a.m. It=E2=80=99s a striking example of the convenience made possible by th=
e gig economy that so many of us rely on to keep our lives running smoothly=
=2E</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;ma=
rgin:0 0 15px"><span>But around the world, the human cost of this ultraconv=
enience is increasingly hard to ignore. To understand what it looks like in=
 one country, I=E2=80=99ve recently turned to a growing body of books, essa=
ys and poems out of China, known as migrant workers=E2=80=99 literature.</s=
pan></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0=
 0 15px"><span>These stories, written from factory floors and, in more rece=
nt years, from the front lines of the gig economy, tell of the harsh realit=
ies of a work force of over 400 million people who moved from the countrysi=
de to cities like Beijing to seek better lives. The stories have started to=
 attract attention outside China, with English translations of books like H=
u Anyan=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9CI Deliver Parcels in Beijing=E2=80=9D and Xiao H=
ai=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9CAdrift in the South.=E2=80=9D</span></p><p style=3D"f=
ont:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>For ma=
ny readers, including me, the attraction is learning about the lives of wor=
kers whose presence is ubiquitous but whose voices are rarely heard. For so=
me Chinese readers, that curiosity is also tinged with anxiety about their =
own futures. The country=E2=80=99s economy </span><a href=3D"https://nl.nyt=
imes.com/f/newsletter/htkNaEpLTMVdljXUSx2K5Q~~/AAAAARA~/t_l8UddsbavyHZdRkPx=
EbYyLBYIf3iQv13yhTqwAMZ3-O_FgQDYi9Kv15lTQjCDZB0njKeBtJefDweqHX47cCpRYbT1Buf=
mOY3-yxUSK9y9CTSDD20mUi5akMkwQKeIQXgIPWrgYwbQnb7V9lV2dCeN0TUUqV3sIQenrzVoGd=
173BrNVGBnL4Mo9uzVUMFTc3kHUH1M8e3fdRe73UUBjFwZ9P9mv0b-kHm8yl5WTPaOHbU-KLjHf=
9pVl74H_cU8_S3RnwDWkw4r_cxxu6xWeQhh4kgddXqCD7rKybP0n6nhzsu6kIC1XPSY7qHyYehc=
sosvmdNeFUN3yigjIX1eC8-epu99z5PryMIsCvpelsTM3ognEGsRcZnSy7Tc905LC" class=3D=
"css-sdwaa1" style=3D"color:#286ed0;-webkit-text-decoration:underline;text-=
decoration:underline">is stagnating</a><span>, jobs are drying up, and the =
once-solid promise that hard work will lead to a better life feels increasi=
ngly uncertain.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;c=
olor:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The genre=E2=80=99s growing popularity has=
 meant more recognition from China=E2=80=99s literary establishment. But th=
at recognition has also come with more state control, from a government tha=
t has always seen literature as political, and that is wary of the power of=
 workers=E2=80=99 grievances to illuminate class disparities.</span></p><p =
style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><s=
pan style=3D"font-weight:700">Masters of the country, once</span></p><p sty=
le=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span=
>Literature by the working class has a long history in China. Under Mao Zed=
ong, the state held up workers and peasants as the masters of society and e=
ncouraged them to =E2=80=9Cspeak bitterness,=E2=80=9D or tell the stories o=
f their past hardships in order to cultivate class consciousness and solidi=
fy allegiance to the Communist regime.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17=
px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But the workers wri=
ting about their experiences today have a much lower standing in society th=
an under Mao, who died in 1976. =E2=80=9CBefore the 1980s, the working clas=
s was proclaimed to be the master of the country,=E2=80=9D said Xin Yang, a=
 professor and expert in Chinese literature at Macalester College. =E2=80=
=9CToday, however, workers have become an underclass under the new socioeco=
nomic order.=E2=80=9D</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,s=
erif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Some of this literature speaks of th=
e hopelessness and despair of being consumed by the backbreaking demands of=
 factory work =E2=80=94 like Xu Lizhi, a 24-year-old Foxconn worker whose p=
oetry was widely circulated after he leaped to his death in 2014. Others ta=
ke a more hopeful tone, writing of how they maintain their humanity in the =
face of dehumanizing labor =E2=80=94 such as Wang Jibing, the 56-year-old f=
ood delivery driver and poet, who in July won the Lu Xun Literary Prize, on=
e of China=E2=80=99s most prestigious literary honors.</span></p><table bor=
der=3D"0" cellpadding=3D"0" cellspacing=3D"0" style=3D"width:100%"><tbody><=
tr><td style=3D"text-align:center"><img width=3D"500" height=3D"625" style=
=3D"display:inline-block;width:83%;max-width:500px;height:auto;padding-top:=
10px;padding-bottom:8px" class=3D"css-18ei5jy" src=3D"https://static01.nyt.=
com/images/2026/09/29/multimedia/29int-theworld-top2-qhpf/29int-theworld-to=
p2-qhpf-jumbo.jpg" alt=3D"A portrait of a man smiling while holding a book.=
"></td></tr><tr><td style=3D"text-align:center;width:100%;padding-bottom:15=
px;line-height:14px" class=3D"css-1b9cqt1"><span style=3D"display:inline-bl=
ock;width:83%;max-width:500px;text-align:left" class=3D"css-14cus5q"><span =
style=3D"color:#666;font:normal 14px georgia,serif">The poet Wang Jibing.=
=C2=A0</span><span style=3D"margin:0;font:11px/11px georgia,serif;color:#88=
8;letter-spacing:.01em">Wang Xuzhong</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table=
><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px=
"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Dignity, resilience =E2=80=94 but no syst=
em critique</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color=
:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Wang=E2=80=99s prize signifies the highest ins=
titutional recognition for the genre yet, and his win has been celebrated w=
idely in Chinese state media for embodying a shift in the literary landscap=
e, in which publishing opportunities are not limited to the elite.</span></=
p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15p=
x"><span>But experts I talked to also noted that Wang=E2=80=99s poems are t=
he kind of politically benign worker narrative the government is keen to pr=
omote. Wang speaks of his desire to be known by his name instead of =E2=80=
=9Cthat guy=E2=80=9D; he describes the experience of always trying to squee=
ze an extra second out of every minute. His poems have a tone of dignity an=
d resilience; they do not, however, focus on inequality, or criticize the s=
ystem.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333=
;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The workers=E2=80=99 literature movement grew organ=
ically over several decades through writers groups and was largely self-pub=
lished in online spaces. The government=E2=80=99s move to bring it into the=
 world of mainstream publishing will create more opportunities for writers,=
 experts told me. But they expect those opportunities will mostly fall to t=
he writers whose perspectives the government wants to highlight.</span></p>=
